# CONV devnodes - device nodes under /dev
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Tier CONVENTION. Sources: FHS 3.0 section 3.6 and chapter 6 (the
# operating-system annex lists the nodes that must exist), hier(7) of the
# three BSD projects (BSD-A, BSD-B, BSD-C as in conv/modules/conv/layout).
#
# What POSIX itself requires of /dev/null, /dev/tty and /dev/console (that
# they exist, and what reading and writing them does) is not repeated here;
# the module adds file type, mode and the nodes POSIX does not mention.
#
# Deliberately not asserted:
#  - /dev/full: none of the four sources lists it
#  - /dev/console: only BSD-B lists it among the sources; POSIX covers it
#  - /dev/ptmx and the exact name of the master: no source names them;
#    only the slave name that ptsname() returns is checked (007)
#  - the mode and owner of a pty slave (no source states them)
#  - /dev/kmem, /dev/mem, /dev/drum, /dev/klog, /dev/log: BSD-B only
#  - the MAKEDEV script FHS 3.6 allows: optional
#  - the device numbers: FHS says there is no requirement

assertion  CONV.devnodes.001
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch06.html#6.1.3
anchor     hier(7)#/dev/null
class      disputed
says       /dev/null, /dev/zero and /dev/tty each exist and are character
           special files (a link to one counts).
test       t/001-char-special.c
note       tier CONVENTION. FHS annex 6.1.3 lists null, zero and tty as devices
           that must exist; BSD-A lists null, BSD-B null, zero and tty. FHS
           and at least one BSD manual agree on each. That they are character
           devices is the meaning of "device file" in both.
nomutant   file-system check; fixture directory via PC_CONV_DEV (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.devnodes.002
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch06.html#6.1.3
anchor     hier(7)#/dev/zero
class      disputed
says       Reading /dev/zero returns as many bytes as requested, all zero;
           writing to it succeeds and discards the data.
test       t/002-zero-reads.c
note       tier CONVENTION. FHS annex 6.1.3 states both properties; BSD-B lists
           the node as the zero device. POSIX does not require /dev/zero.
           Sources agree.
nomutant   file-system check; fixture directory via PC_CONV_DEV (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.devnodes.003
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch06.html#6.1.3
class      disputed
says       /dev/null, /dev/zero and /dev/tty can be read and written by their
           owner, their group and everyone else.
test       t/003-modes.c
note       tier CONVENTION. Sources silent on the mode bits. FHS says any
           process may rely on these nodes (reads and writes of null and
           zero; a synonym for the caller's terminal for tty), which needs
           mode 0666 for an unprivileged caller. Inferred, so a person judges.
nomutant   file-system check; fixture directory via PC_CONV_DEV (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.devnodes.004
anchor     hier(7)#/dev/random
class      disputed
says       Each of /dev/urandom and /dev/random that exists is a character
           special file that can be opened and read without blocking for 32
           bytes twice, and the bytes read are not all alike and not repeated.
test       t/004-random.c
note       tier CONVENTION. Only BSD-A lists a random device ("random"); FHS,
           BSD-B and BSD-C do not mention either. The sources therefore do
           not agree that the nodes exist: existence is not asserted and the
           test is NOTINUSE when neither is there. What is asserted of a node
           that does exist is the behaviour that the name promises. A node
           that would block is UNRESOLVED setup (the kernel has not
           collected entropy yet), not a failure.
nomutant   reads a device; no library call to fault

assertion  CONV.devnodes.005
anchor     hier(7)#/dev/fd
class      disputed
says       /dev/fd is a directory in which /dev/fd/N opens the same open file
           as the process's descriptor N, for the three standard descriptors
           and for one opened by the process.
test       t/005-dev-fd.c
note       tier CONVENTION. All three BSD manuals list /dev/fd/ ("file
           descriptor files"). FHS is silent. Sources agree among the BSD
           manuals. The comparison is by device and inode numbers, so it is
           valid for files, pipes and terminals alike.
nomutant   file-system check; no library call to fault

assertion  CONV.devnodes.006
anchor     hier(7)#/dev/stdin
class      disputed
says       Where /dev/stdin, /dev/stdout and /dev/stderr exist, each names the
           same open file as descriptor 0, 1 and 2.
test       t/006-stdio-names.c
note       tier CONVENTION. Only BSD-B lists the three names; BSD-A and BSD-C
           list /dev/fd/ only (CONV.devnodes.005). The sources disagree that
           the names exist, so absence gives NOTINUSE; the check applies only
           when at least one of the three is present, and then expects all.
nomutant   file-system check; fixture directory via PC_CONV_DEV (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.devnodes.007
anchor     hier(7)#/dev/pts
class      disputed
when       XSI
says       The slave name that ptsname() returns after posix_openpt(), grantpt()
           and unlockpt() begins with /dev/, names an existing character
           special file, and opening it gives a terminal.
test       t/007-pty-names.c
note       tier CONVENTION. BSD-A and BSD-B list /dev/pts/ for pseudo-terminals;
           BSD-C lists none; FHS only says other nodes may exist. The name
           pattern is therefore not asserted (pts/N, ttypN and others are all
           accepted), only that the node is under /dev, which the three
           sources that mention pseudo-terminals place there. POSIX leaves
           the name unspecified. Fixture prefix via PC_CONV_PTY_PREFIX.
nomutant   needs a pseudo-terminal pair; no library call to fault
